# Line endings, pinned.
#
# Every text file here is LF in the index and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform — and that has to be *stated* rather than assumed, because Git for
# Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true (windows-latest does not override the
# installer default, and actions/checkout never touches it), which smudges an
# LF-committed file to CRLF on checkout. gofmt parses CRLF happily and prints LF,
# so it calls every such file unformatted: that is how the Windows CI job once
# failed with 144 findings across the terminal adapter, each pointing at line 1,
# none of them about the code.
#
# Both halves of the mechanism are load-bearing (gitattributes(5)): naming
# `text` explicitly is what takes core.autocrlf out of the answer, and `eol=lf`
# is what decides the checkout. `* text=auto` alone would still normalize the
# index and still hand Windows CRLF, because its eol default there is crlf — the
# half fix that keeps the Linux job green.
#
# `make check-gitattributes` (misc/check-gitattributes.sh) holds these rules to
# it on the platform where a weakened one looks perfectly fine.

# The catch-all: a file type nobody thought about is LF by default, with text
# inferred from content. A fair bet for prose, a bad one for anything executable
# or byte-exact, so those are named below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Typed as text by name: the outcome stops depending on a heuristic noticing that
# a file is text. Shell scripts are the sharp edge — with a CR they do not merely
# look unformatted, they fail to run, and a Makefile fails to build.
*.go        text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.gitignore text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.mod       text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.sum       text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.tape      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
LICENSE     text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# Binary by name, never by detection: adapter-guide/tlv-samples/*.bin are byte
# exact TLV recordings the adapter tests replay, and a fixture that happened to
# read as text would be "corrected" into a different file. The -eol backs out of
# the catch-all's eol=lf, so `git check-attr` does not answer "lf" about a file
# git is not converting at all.
*.bin  binary -eol
*.gif  binary -eol
*.jpg  binary -eol
*.pdf  binary -eol
*.png  binary -eol
*.wav  binary -eol
*.webp binary -eol
